.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-GET-USER-GROUP 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-get-user-group \- show one user group and who is in it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam get-user-group
.BR \-\-group | \-g
.I NAME\fR|\fIERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows one user group as JSON: its ERN, who is in it and when it was
created.
.PP
The same description
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-user-groups (1)
gives of each of its own, for one group.
.PP
Groups are installation-wide rather than scoped to an account, so a name
identifies one on its own. An ERN is accepted too, because that is what a
grant's principal carries \(em so the principal printed by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1)
can be pasted straight back in to find out who the grant actually
reaches.
.PP
Which is the reason to run this. A role granted to a group is held by
every member, and the membership is the part that is not visible in the
grant: the grant names the group, and this is what turns that into
people.
.PP
Requires administrator privileges.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group " " \fINAME\fR|\fIERN\fR
Name of the group, or its full ERN. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The group was found and printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no group of that name, the caller is not an
administrator, or the server could not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eam get\-user\-group \-\-group ftp\-users

euclid\-cli eam get\-user\-group \e
    \-\-group ern:eam:eu\-central\-1:000000000000:userGroup:ftp\-users
.fi
.RE
.PP
What the group may do, which this does not show:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eam list\-grants \e
    \-\-principal ern:eam:eu\-central\-1:000000000000:userGroup:ftp\-users
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
